{{!--
  What `/developer/docs` opens on.

  It used to open on whichever document sorted first alphabetically, which made
  the first impression of the documentation an arbitrary page about one
  integration and told a reader nothing about what else was here.

  The diagram is the one thing worth saying before any of the guides: the same
  holder, the same wallet, proving themselves to three different kinds of
  thing. That is the product in one picture, and it is what every guide below
  is a detail of.
--}}
<div class="docs-landing">

  <header class="docs-landing__hero">
    <h2>One wallet. Every door.</h2>
    <p class="docs-landing__lede">
      Aegis ID is the policy decision point for who may do what, and the Aegis
      wallet is how a person proves it is them. The same credential on the same
      phone opens a desktop sign-in, an app on the phone itself, and a door in
      a building — and every approval and refusal lands on an immutable
      evidence chain.
    </p>
  </header>

  {{!--
    Inline SVG rather than an image: it has to follow the theme, and a picture
    of a diagram goes blurry on a retina screen and illegible in dark mode.
    Every colour comes from the brand tokens for the same reason.
  --}}
  <figure class="docs-landing__figure">
    <svg class="docs-diagram" viewBox="0 0 980 360" role="img"
         aria-labelledby="docs-diagram-title docs-diagram-desc">
      <title id="docs-diagram-title">One wallet proving identity to three kinds of relying party</title>
      <desc id="docs-diagram-desc">
        A credential holder with the Aegis wallet on their phone. The wallet
        answers challenges from Aegis ID, which fronts a desktop application, a
        mobile application, and a physical building. Every answer is written to
        the evidence chain.
      </desc>

      <!-- the holder -->
      <g class="dg-actor">
        <circle class="dg-avatar-ring" cx="92" cy="138" r="40"/>
        <circle class="dg-avatar-head" cx="92" cy="126" r="14"/>
        <path class="dg-avatar-body" d="M66 162c4-15 14-22 26-22s22 7 26 22z"/>
        <!-- the phone in their hand -->
        <rect class="dg-phone" x="118" y="150" width="30" height="50" rx="6"/>
        <rect class="dg-phone-screen" x="123" y="157" width="20" height="33" rx="2"/>
        <path class="dg-phone-check" d="M127.5 173.5l4 4 7.5-8" />
        <text class="dg-label" x="92" y="222" text-anchor="middle">Credential holder</text>
        <text class="dg-sub" x="92" y="240" text-anchor="middle">Aegis wallet</text>
      </g>

      <!-- holder to platform -->
      <path class="dg-flow" d="M158 170 H300"/>
      <text class="dg-flow-label" x="229" y="158" text-anchor="middle">challenge</text>
      <text class="dg-flow-label" x="229" y="192" text-anchor="middle">approve · decline</text>

      <!-- the platform -->
      <g class="dg-platform">
        <rect class="dg-platform-box" x="302" y="112" width="188" height="116" rx="14"/>
        <text class="dg-platform-title" x="396" y="152" text-anchor="middle">Aegis ID</text>
        <text class="dg-sub" x="396" y="174" text-anchor="middle">policy decision point</text>
        <text class="dg-sub" x="396" y="192" text-anchor="middle">deny by default</text>
      </g>

      <!-- platform to the three relying parties -->
      <path class="dg-flow" d="M492 170 H560 V74 H612"/>
      <path class="dg-flow" d="M492 170 H612"/>
      <path class="dg-flow" d="M492 170 H560 V268 H612"/>

      <!-- desktop -->
      <g class="dg-rp" transform="translate(620 36)">
        <rect class="dg-rp-box" x="0" y="0" width="300" height="76" rx="12"/>
        <g class="dg-glyph" transform="translate(22 20)">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="34" height="24" rx="3"/>
          <path d="M11 30h12M17 24v6"/>
        </g>
        <text class="dg-rp-title" x="72" y="32">Desktop application</text>
        <text class="dg-sub" x="72" y="52">OIDC · SAML · connected apps</text>
      </g>

      <!-- mobile -->
      <g class="dg-rp" transform="translate(620 132)">
        <rect class="dg-rp-box" x="0" y="0" width="300" height="76" rx="12"/>
        <g class="dg-glyph" transform="translate(28 16)">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="22" height="34" rx="4"/>
          <path d="M8 29h6"/>
        </g>
        <text class="dg-rp-title" x="72" y="32">Mobile application</text>
        <text class="dg-sub" x="72" y="52">passkeys · wallet approval</text>
      </g>

      <!-- building -->
      <g class="dg-rp" transform="translate(620 230)">
        <rect class="dg-rp-box" x="0" y="0" width="300" height="76" rx="12"/>
        <g class="dg-glyph" transform="translate(22 18)">
          <path d="M2 30V8l14-6 14 6v22"/>
          <path d="M11 30v-9h10v9"/>
          <path d="M11 14h3M18 14h3"/>
        </g>
        <text class="dg-rp-title" x="72" y="32">Physical access</text>
        <text class="dg-sub" x="72" y="52">a door, a desk, a controlled room</text>
      </g>

      <!-- evidence -->
      <g class="dg-evidence">
        <path class="dg-flow dg-flow--muted" d="M396 228 V292 H306"/>
        <rect class="dg-evidence-box" x="96" y="268" width="206" height="48" rx="10"/>
        <text class="dg-evidence-text" x="199" y="290" text-anchor="middle">Evidence chain</text>
        <text class="dg-sub" x="199" y="306" text-anchor="middle">every approval and refusal, immutable</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      The wallet answers; Aegis decides; the ledger remembers. A decline is
      recorded exactly as an approval is.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="docs-landing__pillars" aria-label="What Aegis ID does">
    <article class="docs-pillar">
      <span class="docs-pillar__icon" aria-hidden="true">{{agIcon "shield" 20}}</span>
      <h3>Authorization, centralized</h3>
      <p>
        One policy registry, enforced server-side on every route. An
        integration is an adapter in front of it, never a second place where
        access is decided.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="docs-pillar">
      <span class="docs-pillar__icon" aria-hidden="true">{{agIcon "phone" 20}}</span>
      <h3>Wallet-backed assurance</h3>
      <p>
        A challenge goes to the holder's own device and comes back signed and
        bound to their Wallet ID. Approve and decline are both meaningful, and
        both are recorded.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="docs-pillar">
      <span class="docs-pillar__icon" aria-hidden="true">{{agIcon "badge" 20}}</span>
      <h3>Credentials you can revoke</h3>
      <p>
        Issue, consent, revoke. A revoked wallet stops working at acceptance
        rather than merely looking disabled, and the trail it leaves behind
        stays.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="docs-pillar">
      <span class="docs-pillar__icon" aria-hidden="true">{{agIcon "key" 20}}</span>
      <h3>Hardware-backed sign-in</h3>
      <p>
        WebAuthn, passkeys and YubiKey. A passkey with user verification is
        possession plus inherence, so it finishes a sign-in on its own — a
        password never does.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-landing__start" aria-label="Where to start">
    <h3>Where to start</h3>
    <div class="docs-start-grid">
      {{#each categories}}
        {{#if count}}
          <a class="docs-start-card" href="{{#with documents.[0]}}{{href}}{{/with}}">
            <strong>{{title}}</strong>
            <span>{{summary}}</span>
            <small>{{count}} {{#if (eq count 1)}}guide{{else}}guides{{/if}}</small>
          </a>
        {{/if}}
      {{/each}}
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
